Weekly tips on front-end & UX.Trusted by 182,000+ folks.Replacing JavaScript with a modern CSS feature can simplify a piece of code while making the wider application harder to reason about. Gabriel Shoyombo explores what happens when logic is split between CSS and JavaScript, sharing practical guidance to help you keep complexity in check during migration.Have you ever replaced fifty lines of JavaScript with a few lines of CSS, merged the PR, and felt like you just did the codebase a favor? You replace ResizeObserver with a container query, the diff is mostly red, and everyone approves it before lunch.This was what it looked like before you made the change:See the Pen [demo (before PR) [forked]](https://codepen.io/smashingmag/pen/XJMxVJq) by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.See the Pen demo (before PR) [forked] by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.Then, a week later, a card on the dashboard has vanished. A heading has grown on tablets for no reason anyone can explain. The spacing inside a comment is off.See the Pen [demo (after PR) [forked]](https://codepen.io/smashingmag/pen/gbmBopp) by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.See the Pen demo (after PR) [forked] by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.None of these bugs are in the few lines you wrote. All of them arrived with those few lines of CSS.In my last article on how CSS is growing smarter, I left a few questions hanging at the end, including: What happens when logic is split between CSS and JavaScript, and how do we debug conditional styles without a clear view of what triggered them? This article is my attempt to answer these two questions.I think it’s worth saying clearly that I am not saying modern CSS is bad. What I am saying is that:A CSS feature can simplify a piece of code while making the wider application harder to reason about if you don’t understand how it relates to existing code, be it CSS or JavaScript.“Local Simplicity Vs. System ComplexityThink of a new CSS feature as a new tenant moving into a shared apartment. The tenant may be quiet and tidy, but they still share a kitchen with everyone who already lives there. In that case, CSS is now roommates with JavaScript, the component’s layout, the CSS Cascade, and the browser’s rendering engine. The apartment’s stability depends less on the tenant and more on how they get along with one another.That gives us two different things to measure, and a diff only shows the first one:Local simplicity: How little code a single file needs.System complexity: The number of interactions you must understand before you can safely change the application.I am not the only one thinking about this. In April 2026, Igor Roztropiński published an essay titled “Modern Frontend Complexity: essential or accidental?” asking:“Can we start from scratch and figure out a much simpler approach, given how browsers have evolved in recent years?”— Igor RoztropińskiThe essay landed on r/programming, where the thread has drawn 80-plus comments and the replies pull in two directions. One of the most upvoted comments, from a user named Kwantuum, pushes back on the whole genre:“All these articles are like ‘We can do it with less!’ but then they don’t do it with less. They just do less.”— KwantuumAnother commenter, kayinfire, takes the other side, arguing that “there are tons of scenarios where a front-end framework introduces more accidental complexity than it’s worth.” They say this a few replies later:“I don’t even need to go into how massively CSS has improved interactivity over the recent years, but hey nobody cares about anything apart from JS anymore, which I find to be quite a shame.”— Keyser SözeI think both commenters are on to something, and this article lives in the gap between the two. Yes, CSS has improved in the last several years. And yes, “less” CSS on paper is not automatically “less” complexity in practice. That said, my claim is narrower. A native feature only reduces complexity when it actually replaces what was there before and doesn’t disrupt the calm state of everything else it interacts with.Let’s meet the housemates in our metaphorical apartment, one scenario at a time.Scenario 1: The Behavior With Two OwnersThe first housemate is JavaScript, and the trouble starts when the old tenant never quite moves out. Here is a card that switches to a two-column layout when it has enough room. The original version uses a ResizeObserver to toggle a class:const card = document.querySelector('.card');
new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
card.classList.toggle('card--wide', entry.contentRect.width > 480);
}).observe(card);
.card--wide .card__body {
display: grid;
grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr;
}
.card--wide .card__title {
font-size: 1.5rem;
}
With CSS Container Queries, we need no script at all and can simplify our selectors at the same time:
.card {
container-type: inline-size;
}
@container (width > 480px) {
.card__body {
display: grid;
grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr;
}
}
Lovely. Now, here’s the thing: migrations rarely happen in one clean sweep. The pull request adds the @container rule and removes the .card--wide .card__body rule. The observer survives because it is registered in another file. The .card--wide .card__title rule survives because nobody searched for the class. Nothing looks broken, because both implementations agree on 480px. Then a designer asks to move the breakpoint to 600px, and a developer updates the only place they know about: the @container rule.See the Pen [Scenario 1: The Behaviour With Two Owners [forked]](https://codepen.io/smashingmag/pen/ZYeqvpx) by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.See the Pen Scenario 1: The Behaviour With Two Owners [forked] by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.Between 480px and 600px, the card now has a single-column body and a wide-layout title. Nobody wrote that state. To find it, you need to know that the layout has two owners and read both of them.The same trap comes in other shapes:JavaScript toggles an .is-active class while :has() derives the very same state from the DOM.A JavaScript animation and a CSS transition both respond to one interaction.Old media-query rules stay beside the new container-query rules, so the component answers to the viewport in one place and to its container in another.CSS and JavaScript can happily share a component. What they cannot share is the component’s behavior. So, instead of having a silent fight over a component’s behavior, check first. Know all the contending parties, then split the work.CSS owns presentation that follows from size or DOM structure.The layout at a given width. The appearance of a form containing an invalid field.JavaScript owns application state and asynchronous work.Whether a request is pending. What the server returned. Anything that code outside the stylesheet needs to read.And when you’re done migrating, you have to finish the job: delete the observer, the class, and every selector that mentions the class. Otherwise, you may wind up with a tenant who simply refuses to move out.Scenario 2: The Roommate Rearranges The RoomBack in 2023, I wrote about how block formatting contexts solve float issues. Back then, creating a block formatting context was something we did on purpose. Today, you can create one without meaning to. When we declare container-type: inline-size, we think we are saying, “let my children query my width.” The browser hears quite a bit more. This is the second housemate, the layout around the component, and our new tenant who just asked for one shelf has ended up rearranging the living room.According to MDN’s container-type reference, the value “applies style and inline-size containment to the element.” And MDN’s guide to block formatting contexts lists “query containers (elements where container-type isn’t normal)” among the CSS styles that create one. An element that establishes a new block formatting context will, in MDN’s words:Contain internal floats,Exclude external floats, andSuppress margin collapsing.That means one declaration can change three layout behaviors that have nothing to do with queries, plus a fourth that comes from inline-size containment. I built a demo where each “before” and “after” pair differs by only that one declaration.See the Pen [Scenario 2: The Card That Vanished [forked]](https://codepen.io/smashingmag/pen/dPvgJpB) by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.See the Pen Scenario 2: The Card That Vanished [forked] by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.Margins Stop CollapsingA paragraph with margin-bottom: 16px sits above the card, and the card’s heading has margin-top: 24px. Before the change, the heading’s margin collapsed through the top of the card and merged with the paragraph’s margin, leaving a 24px gap. Afterward, the margin remained trapped inside the card. The two margins now add up to 40px, and the card grows from 30px to 54px tall.Floats Push The Card AsideThe card follows a floated image. Before the change, the card’s box ran behind the float and its text wrapped around the image. Afterward, the whole card sits beside the float; it starts 120px further in (the width of the image) and is 120px narrower.Content-Sized Cards CollapseThis is the one that makes cards vanish. Inline-size containment stops an element from taking its width from its contents. MDN has a nice, succinct explanation:“If a contextual or explicit size is not available, elements with size containment will collapse.”— MDNIn the last demo, three tag-sized cards sit in a flex row and measure roughly 74px, 96px, and 114px, respectively. If we add container-type: inline-size, then all three measure 0px. I got the same 0px result when the card was displayed inline-block, floated, set to a width: fit-content box, was an align-items: flex-start child of a column flex container, and when it was an item in an auto grid track. A plain block-level card retains its full width because its width is determined by its parent rather than its contents.In isolation, the card is a block that fills its parent. In reality, it is a flex item, follows a float, or sits under a paragraph whose margin it used to absorb.Before you add container-type to an element, ask yourself the following three questions:Where does its width come from?If the answer is “its contents,” give it a width from context (e.g., flex: 1 and width: 100%), or move container-type to a wrapper that already has one.Do its first or last children have margins?Those margins may be collapsing through the element.Where is it actually used?Test the component inside every real layout that hosts it, not just in isolation.Answering these will help prevent a housemate from completely changing the furniture layout right from under you.Scenario 3: The Loud HousemateThe third housemate is the CSS Cascade. Every shared apartment has house rules about who gets the last word, and a new tenant can arrive with more say than anyone expected.The :has() selector and native nesting let us write short selectors in CSS. But short selectors can match more and weigh more than they appear.Say we want to remove paragraph margins from card components that have a hero image. This might look like it does the job:<article class="card">
<p>Body text of the card.</p>
<p>A second paragraph.</p>
<div class="comment">
<img src="avatar.png" alt="">
<p>Nice post.</p>
</div>
</article>
.card:has(img) p {
margin: 0;
}
/* Elsewhere, the comment component's own rule */
.comment p {
margin-block: 8px;
}
See the Pen [Scenario 3: The Selector That Reached Too Far [forked]](https://codepen.io/smashingmag/pen/pvexpRd) by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.See the Pen Scenario 3: The Selector That Reached Too Far [forked] by Shoyombo Gabriel Ayomide.This card has no hero image. Yet every paragraph in it loses its margin.If you inspect the comment’s paragraph in DevTools a

